Structured state space sequence models (S4) are a recent class of sequence models for deep learning that are broadly related to RNNs, CNNs, and classical state space models. They are inspired by a particular continuous system (1) that maps a 1-dimensional sequence $x\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathtt{T}}\mapsto y\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathtt{T}}$ through an implicit latent state $h\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathtt{(T,N)}}$.

A general discrete form of structured SSMs takes the form of equation (1).

\displaystyle h_{t} \displaystyle=Ah_{t-1}+Bx_{t} \displaystyle y_{t} \displaystyle=C^{\top}h_{t}

\displaystyle h_{t} \displaystyle=A_{t}h_{t-1}+B_{t}x_{t} \displaystyle y_{t} \displaystyle=C_{t}^{\top}h_{t}

where $A\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathtt{(N,N)}},B\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathtt{(N,1)}},C\in\mathbb{R}^{\mathtt{(N,1)}}$. Structured SSMs are so named because the $A$ matrix controlling the temporal dynamics must be structured in order to compute this sequence-to-sequence transformation efficiently enough to be used in deep neural networks. The original structures introduced were diagonal plus low-rank (DPLR) (Gu et al., 2022) and diagonal (Gupta et al., 2022; Gu et al., 2022a; Smith et al., 2023), which remains the most popular structure.

